::GEO_U03_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 48&deg; and 67&deg;. What is the third angle? {
=A. 65&deg;#48 + 67 = 115, and 180 - 115 = 65.
~B. 55&deg;#This subtracts incorrectly from 180&deg;.
~C. 75&deg;#These angles would total 190&deg;.
~D. 115&deg;#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 2x&deg;, 3x&deg;, and 4x&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 18#This makes the sum 162&deg;, not 180&deg;.
=B. 20#2x + 3x + 4x = 180, so 9x = 180 and x = 20.
~C. 30#This makes the sum 270&deg;.
~D. 45#This makes the sum 405&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is 110&deg;. One remote interior angle is 45&deg;. What is the other remote interior angle? {
~A. 55&deg;#45 + 55 does not equal 110.
~B. 70&deg;#45 + 70 equals 115, not 110.
=C. 65&deg;#By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 45 + x = 110, so x = 65.
~D. 155&deg;#This adds instead of subtracting.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which theorem explains why triangle interior angles add to 180&deg;? {
~A. Exterior Angle Theorem#This theorem relates an exterior angle to two remote interior angles.
~B. Vertical Angles Theorem#Vertical angles are formed by intersecting lines.
~C. Pythagorean Theorem#The Pythagorean Theorem relates side lengths in right triangles.
=D. Triangle Angle Sum Theorem#This theorem states that the interior angles of a triangle sum to 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which angles are remote interior angles for an exterior angle? {
=A. The two interior angles not adjacent to the exterior angle.#Remote interior angles are the two nonadjacent interior angles.
~B. The exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle.#The adjacent interior angle is not remote.
~C. Only the largest angle.#Remote status depends on position, not size.
~D. Any two angles that look equal.#Appearance does not define remote interior angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles x&deg;, (x + 20)&deg;, and 70&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 35#This makes the total 160&deg;.
=B. 45#x + x + 20 + 70 = 180, so 2x = 90 and x = 45.
~C. 55#This makes the total 200&deg;.
~D. 90#This treats x as the whole missing total.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Remote interior angles are 38&deg; and 72&deg;. What is the exterior angle? {
~A. 100&deg;#The sum is 110&deg;, not 100&deg;.
~B. 34&deg;#This subtracts instead of adding.
=C. 110&deg;#The exterior angle equals 38 + 72 = 110&deg;.
~D. 180&deg;#180&deg; is not the exterior angle here.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 60&deg;, 60&deg;, and 60&deg;. What relationship is shown? {
~A. The exterior angle equals 60&deg;.#The item gives interior angles, not an exterior angle.
~B. The angle sum is 120&deg;.#The sum is 180&deg;, not 120&deg;.
~C. One angle is obtuse.#No angle is greater than 90&deg;.
=D. The angles satisfy the Triangle Angle Sum Theorem.#60 + 60 + 60 = 180, matching the theorem.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 33&deg;, 82&deg;, and x&deg;. Which equation is correct? {
=A. 33 + 82 + x = 180#The three interior angles sum to 180&deg;.
~B. 33 + 82 = x#This makes x the sum of two known angles.
~C. 33 + x = 82#This ignores the 180&deg; total.
~D. 180 + x = 33 + 82#This places 180&deg; on the wrong side.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is 130&deg;. What is true about the two remote interior angles together? {
~A. They sum to 50&deg;.#50&deg; is the adjacent interior angle in the linear pair.
=B. They sum to 130&deg;.#The Exterior Angle Theorem says the remote interior angles sum to the exterior angle.
~C. They sum to 180&deg;.#180&deg; is the total of all interior angles.
~D. They are each 130&deg;.#The theorem gives a sum, not each angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 74&deg; and 39&deg;. What is the missing angle? {
~A. 57&deg;#This does not make the total 180&deg;.
~B. 77&deg;#This makes the total 190&deg;.
=C. 67&deg;#74 + 39 = 113 and 180 - 113 = 67.
~D. 113&deg;#This is the sum of the given angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles x&deg;, x&deg;, and 80&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 40#This gives a total of 160&deg;.
~B. 80#This gives a total of 240&deg;.
~C. 100#This gives a total of 280&deg;.
=D. 50#2x + 80 = 180, so 2x = 100 and x = 50.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is 142&deg;. One remote interior angle is 68&deg;. Find the other remote interior angle. {
=A. 74&deg;#68 + x = 142, so x = 74.
~B. 64&deg;#68 + 64 = 132, not 142.
~C. 82&deg;#68 + 82 = 150, not 142.
~D. 210&deg;#This adds the exterior angle and remote angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles (x + 10)&deg;, (x + 30)&deg;, and 80&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 20#This makes the angle sum 160&deg;.
=B. 30#x + 10 + x + 30 + 80 = 180, so 2x + 120 = 180 and x = 30.
~C. 40#This makes the angle sum 200&deg;.
~D. 60#This makes the angle sum 240&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle form what total? {
~A. 90&deg;#90&deg; only applies if the pair is specifically complementary, which is not given.
~B. The exterior angle only#The adjacent interior angle must be included.
=C. 180&deg;#They form a linear pair, so their measures sum to 180&deg;.
~D. The two remote interior angles only#Remote interior angles relate to the exterior angle, not the adjacent linear-pair total.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>If an exterior angle is 118&deg;, what is the adjacent interior angle? {
~A. 72&deg;#72&deg; does not complete the linear pair to 180&deg;.
~B. 118&deg;#Adjacent and exterior angles are not generally equal.
~C. 180&deg;#180&deg; is the total, not the adjacent angle.
=D. 62&deg;#The exterior and adjacent interior angle form a linear pair: 180 - 118 = 62.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has interior angles 2x&deg;, (x + 15)&deg;, and 75&deg;. What is x? {
=A. 30#2x + x + 15 + 75 = 180, so 3x + 90 = 180 and x = 30.
~B. 25#This gives a total of 165&deg;.
~C. 35#This gives a total of 195&deg;.
~D. 45#This gives a total of 225&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is 96&deg;. The remote interior angles are x&deg; and 2x&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 24#This gives a remote interior sum of 72&deg;.
=B. 32#x + 2x = 96, so 3x = 96 and x = 32.
~C. 48#This gives a remote interior sum of 144&deg;.
~D. 64#This gives a remote interior sum of 192&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly uses the Exterior Angle Theorem? {
~A. Exterior angle = adjacent interior angle#The adjacent interior angle forms a linear pair with the exterior angle.
~B. Exterior angle = sum of all three interior angles#All three interior angles sum to 180&deg;, not the exterior angle.
=C. Exterior angle = sum of two remote interior angles#Correct. This is the Exterior Angle Theorem.
~D. Exterior angle = half of a remote interior angle#The theorem does not use half of a remote angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has two angles measuring 41&deg; and 99&deg;. What is the third angle? {
~A. 50&deg;#This makes the total 190&deg;.
~B. 58&deg;#This makes the total 198&deg;.
~C. 140&deg;#This is the sum of the known angles.
=D. 40&deg;#41 + 99 = 140, and 180 - 140 = 40.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles (3x)&deg;, (x + 20)&deg;, and 40&deg;. What is x? {
=A. 30#3x + x + 20 + 40 = 180, so 4x + 60 = 180 and x = 30.
~B. 25#This gives a total of 160&deg;.
~C. 35#This gives a total of 200&deg;.
~D. 45#This gives a total of 240&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is 150&deg;. One remote interior angle is 85&deg;. What is the other remote interior angle? {
~A. 55&deg;#85 + 55 = 140, not 150.
=B. 65&deg;#85 + x = 150, so x = 65.
~C. 75&deg;#85 + 75 = 160, not 150.
~D. 235&deg;#This adds instead of finding the missing remote interior angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation represents an exterior angle of 125&deg; with remote interior angles x&deg; and 47&deg;? {
~A. x + 47 = 180#180&deg; is the triangle interior sum or linear-pair total, not this exterior relationship.
~B. x = 125 + 47#This adds when the relationship should set the sum equal to 125.
=C. x + 47 = 125#The exterior angle equals the sum of the remote interior angles.
~D. 125 + x = 47#This makes the larger exterior part of the smaller angle relationship.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 28&deg;, 64&deg;, and x&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 78&deg;#This gives a total of 170&deg;.
~B. 98&deg;#This gives a total of 190&deg;.
~C. 92&deg;#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle.
=D. 88&deg;#28 + 64 = 92, and 180 - 92 = 88.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why must a triangle angle solution include an equation or theorem? {
=A. It shows the conclusion follows from a valid triangle relationship.#Correct. The equation or theorem is the evidence for the conclusion.
~B. It makes the answer longer.#Longer work is not automatically better; the reason must be valid.
~C. It replaces the need to calculate.#You still need to calculate accurately.
~D. It proves the diagram is drawn to scale.#Diagrams are not assumed to be drawn to scale.
}

